# Line-ending normalization — cross-platform correctness invariant (Law 2: Every Environment).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (proven root cause, 2026-08-03, MASTER-CLI-SYMLINK-FLAKE-001):
#
# Without it, Git for Windows (which ships `core.autocrlf=true`, and which the GitHub
# `windows-latest` runner image inherits) checks out every tracked text file with CRLF endings.
# That silently breaks Vitest for any test that imports one of this repo's 93 shebang-bearing
# scripts:
#
#   1. Vite's SSR transform locates the shebang with `/^#!.*\n/`. In JavaScript, `.` does not
#      match `\r` (CR is a line terminator), so on a CRLF tree the pattern never matches and the
#      computed file-start offset stays 0.
#   2. Hoisted `__vite_ssr_import__` statements are therefore inserted at offset 0 — ABOVE the
#      shebang instead of below it.
#   3. The downstream module runner blanks the shebang only when the transformed code still
#      STARTS with `#`. It no longer does, so `#!` survives into the evaluated function body and
#      the module dies with `SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token`.
#
# Reproduced and ablated on Linux, not inferred: converting `scripts/lint-master-plan.mjs` to
# CRLF reproduces the exact Windows failure, and removing only its shebang (CRLF untouched)
# turns the same suite green 73/73.
#
# `eol=lf` in this file overrides `core.autocrlf` regardless of the runner or developer machine
# setting, which is what makes the fix hold on every host rather than on ones we remembered to
# configure. The repository index is already all-LF (`git ls-files --eol`), so this normalizes
# checkout behaviour without rewriting any stored blob.
#
# Existing Windows/CRLF working copies do not fix themselves: re-checkout with
# `git rm --cached -r . && git reset --hard` after pulling this file.

# Detect text automatically (never force a rule onto a file Git sees as binary — this repo has
# at least one tracked `.md` containing NUL bytes that must NOT be normalized), and give every
# detected text file LF in the index AND in the working tree on all platforms.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt-and-braces binaries: byte-exactness matters more here than auto-detection being right.
*.png    -text
*.svg    text eol=lf
*.woff2  -text
*.db     -text
*.db-shm -text
*.db-wal -text
# Signing/verification key material — must stay byte-identical.
*.ed25519 -text
